# Code Formatting Conventions

## Parameter Alignment Rules

### General Principle

**When a method, constructor, or annotation has more than 2 parameters, place each parameter on its own line. For short parameter lists (1-2 params) that fit within the line width (120 chars), keep them on the same line.**

### Key Rules

1. **Every closing bracket on its own line** - When an annotation/method is wrapped, the closing `)` goes on a new line
2. **Each annotation on its own line** - Multiple annotations on a parameter/field are each on separate lines
3. **Field/variable on its own line** - After annotations, the field declaration goes on a new line

### Annotations with Multiple Parameters

When an annotation has more than 2 parameters, wrap with each on its own line:

```java
// GOOD - Each param and closing bracket on own line
@Schema(
		description = "ISO 6346 container number (4 uppercase letters + 7 digits)",
		example = "MSCU1234567",
		pattern = "^[A-Z]{4}[0-9]{7}$",
		minLength = 11,
		maxLength = 11,
		requiredMode = Schema.RequiredMode.REQUIRED
)
@NotBlank(message = "Container number is required")
@Size(min = 11, max = 11, message = "Container number must be exactly 11 characters")
@Pattern(
		regexp = "^[A-Z]{4}[0-9]{7}$",
		message = "Container number must be in ISO 6346 format (4 letters + 7 digits)"
)
String containerNumber;  // Field on its own line

// GOOD - Short annotation (fits on one line)
@Size(min = 11, max = 11)
@NotBlank(message = "Required")
String value;
```

### Multiple Annotations on Parameters

Each annotation must be on its own line:

```java
// GOOD - Each annotation on separate line, field at the end
@Schema(description = "Container number", example = "MSCU1234567")
@NotBlank(message = "Required")
@Size(min = 11, max = 11)
String containerNumber;

// BAD - Annotations chained on same line
@Schema(...) @NotBlank(...) @Size(...) String containerNumber;
```

### Method Invocations

When a method call has more than 2 arguments, wrap each on its own line with closing paren on its own line:

```java
// GOOD - Each argument on its own line, closing paren on new line
var response = new ContainerSummaryResponse(
		summary.containerId(),
		summary.containerNumber(),
		summary.containerStatus(),
		summary.terminalCode()
);

// GOOD - 2 or fewer arguments: same line
var result = someMethod(firstArgument, secondArgument);
```

### Record Components

Records wrap each component on its own line when they have annotations:

```java
// GOOD - Each annotation and field on its own line
public record ContainerLookupRequest(
		@Schema(
				description = "Container number",
				example = "MSCU1234567"
		)
		@NotBlank
		String containerNumber,

		@Schema(description = "Terminal code", nullable = true)
		String terminalCode
) {}

// GOOD - Simple record with few short components
public record Point(int x, int y) {}
```

### Method/Constructor Parameters

Method declarations wrap parameters when more than 2 or exceeding line width:

```java
// GOOD - More than 2 parameters: each on its own line
public void processContainer(
		String containerNumber,
		String terminalCode,
		LocalDateTime timestamp
) {
	// implementation
}

// GOOD - 2 or fewer parameters: same line
public void processContainer(String containerNumber, String terminalCode) {
	// implementation
}
```

### Array Initializers in Annotations

When annotations use array initializers (e.g., `@ApiResponses`), each element goes on its own line:

```java
// GOOD - Each element on its own line with braces on separate lines
@ApiResponses(
		{
				@ApiResponse(responseCode = "200", description = "Success"),
				@ApiResponse(responseCode = "400", description = "Bad request"),
				@ApiResponse(responseCode = "503", description = "Service unavailable")
		}
)
public ResponseEntity<?> lookup() { ... }

// BAD - Elements inline after opening brace
@ApiResponses(
	{@ApiResponse(...), @ApiResponse(...)}
)
```

### Builder/Fluent API Chains

Each method call in a fluent chain should be on its own line:

```java
// GOOD
var client = RestClient.builder()
		.baseUrl("https://api.example.com")
		.defaultHeader("Accept", "application/json")
		.connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
		.build();
```

## Spotless Enforcement

These rules are enforced by the Spotless Maven plugin with Eclipse formatter configuration.

### Eclipse Formatter Settings

The following settings in `eclipse-formatter.xml` enforce these conventions:

| Setting | Value | Description |
|---------|-------|-------------|
| `alignment_for_arguments_in_annotation` | `80` | Force wrap annotation arguments one per line |
| `alignment_for_arguments_in_method_invocation` | `80` | Force wrap method arguments one per line |
| `alignment_for_annotations_on_parameter` | `49` | Wrap each annotation on parameter to new line |
| `alignment_for_record_components` | `81` | Force one per line for record components |
| `parentheses_positions_in_annotation` | `separate_lines_if_wrapped` | Put parens on separate lines when wrapped |
| `parentheses_positions_in_method_invocation` | `separate_lines_if_wrapped` | Put parens on separate lines when wrapped |
| `insert_new_line_after_annotation_on_parameter` | `insert` | New line after annotations before field |
| `alignment_for_expressions_in_array_initializer` | `80` | Force wrap array elements one per line |
| `insert_new_line_after_opening_brace_in_array_initializer` | `insert` | New line after `{` in arrays |
| `insert_new_line_before_closing_brace_in_array_initializer` | `insert` | New line before `}` in arrays |
| `lineSplit` | `120` | Maximum line width before wrapping |

**Alignment values:**
- `16` = wrap one per line when line exceeds width
- `49` = wrap all except first + wrap one per line + wrap (32 + 16 + 1)
- `80` = force split + wrap one per line (64 + 16)
- `81` = force split + wrap one per line + wrap (64 + 16 + 1)

### Running Spotless

```bash
# Check formatting (fails if not compliant)
mvn spotless:check

# Apply formatting automatically
mvn spotless:apply
```

## Continuation Indentation

When parameters wrap, they use **continuation indentation** (2 tabs by default):

```java
@Schema(
		description = "Container number",
		example = "MSCU1234567",
		pattern = "^[A-Z]{4}[0-9]{7}$"
)
String containerNumber;
```

## Exception: Short Annotations

Single-element annotations or annotations with 1-2 short parameters that fit within line width remain on one line:

```java
// GOOD - Short annotations stay on one line
@NotBlank
@Size(max = 100)
@Override
@Deprecated(since = "2.0")
@Schema(description = "User ID")
@Size(min = 11, max = 11, message = "Must be 11 chars")
```

